# Styling with Tailwind CSS

Use **Tailwind utility classes** for layout, spacing, and custom styling. Prefer Tailwind over custom CSS or inline styles.

## Core Guidelines

- **Use utility classes in templates**: Apply Tailwind classes directly in HTML (e.g. `class="flex flex-col gap-4"`).
- **Mobile-first**: Use responsive breakpoints (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`) to add styles for larger screens.
- **Consistent spacing**: Use Tailwind's scale (`p-4`, `m-2`, `gap-4`, `space-y-4`) instead of arbitrary values.
- **Layout**: Prefer `flex` and `grid` with `gap-*` for alignment and spacing.

## Do

```html
<!-- Layout and spacing -->
<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <section class="p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm">...</section>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Responsive -->
<div class="text-sm md:text-base lg:text-lg">...</div>
```

## Don't

- Avoid inline `style=""` when a Tailwind utility exists.
- Avoid arbitrary values (`p-[13px]`) unless the design system does not cover the need.
- Don't override PrimeNG component structure with Tailwind; use Tailwind for layout and spacing around PrimeNG components.

## With PrimeNG

- Use Tailwind for **layout** (containers, grids, gaps) and **spacing** around PrimeNG components.
- Use PrimeNG's own styling/theming for buttons, inputs, and tables.
- Use `::ng-deep` only when necessary to tweak PrimeNG internals; prefer wrapping with Tailwind classes.
